// A WEBASSEMBLY MODULE THAT IMPORTS NOTHING, built by `polaron build` rather than by hand.
//
// `run_wasm_link_test.cmake` already drove polc -> clang -> wasm-ld directly, and because it did, two
// gaps stayed invisible for as long as wasm existed as a target:
//
//   1. `polaron build` could not produce a module at all. The freestanding path linked with `ld.lld`
//      unconditionally, and a wasm object is not an ELF: "unknown file type", once per object. Every
//      other part of the toolchain was ready; the whole failure was in the driver, exactly as it was
//      for the AArch64 port.
//
//   2. The entry wrapper marshalled `argv`. The test for "is this bare metal" was the substring
//      `none` in the triple, and `wasm32-unknown-unknown` does not contain it -- so a module with no
//      libc, no argv and nothing to call `main` took the HOSTED path and came out importing
//      `__polaron_malloc` and `strlen` purely to build a `string[]` from a command line that does not
//      exist. The question is "is there a C runtime", and now one method answers it for both the
//      declaration and the body of the entry point.
//
// What is exercised below is deliberately not just arithmetic. A region class puts the narrow 32-bit
// `A*` in a wasm module -- its arena is the static one the driver emits for freestanding, so both
// halves of the contract have to hold here too: one base, and offset zero never handed out. Contracts
// are in because their failure path is a call the module would otherwise have to import.
//
// The test asserts the module has NO undefined symbols. A wasm import is not a broken image the way an
// undefined ELF symbol is -- it is a demand on the host -- so "it links" proves nothing about
// self-containment and the symbol table is the only thing that does.
program WasmModule freestanding;

public bundle Main {
    public namespace App {

        public region class Node {
            public mutable int value;
            public mutable nullable Node* next;

            invariant this.value >= 0;

            public constructor Node(int value) {
                this.value = value;
                this.next = null;
            }

            public method total() returns int {
                if (this.next == null) { return this.value; }
                return this.value + this.next.total();
            }
        }

        public class Main {
            public static method main(string[] args) returns void {
                Node* head = new Node(10);
                head.next = new Node(20);
                head.next.next = new Node(12);
                // 42, and it is only 42 if each narrow pointer read back as the object stored.
                head.value = head.total();
                return;
            }
        }
    }
}
